# KNOWN GAP — PARSER. An INLINE `case` arm body accepts an EXPRESSION but not a
# STATEMENT, while an inline `if` body accepts either.
#
#     if d == 1: name = "Mon"        # ACCEPTED
#     case 1:    name = "Mon"        # REJECTED: expected 'case', found '='
#     case 1:    "Mon"               # ACCEPTED (expression arm)
#     case 1:                        # ACCEPTED (block arm)
#         name = "Mon"
#
# So the axis is INLINE-vs-BLOCK arm body, not `if`-vs-`case`: both constructs
# are fine in block form, and `if` is fine inline. Only the inline `case` body
# is restricted, and the restriction is a PARSE error naming `case`, which gives
# the reader no hint that the fix is to indent the body onto the next line.
#
# ⚠ OWNER RULING 2026-08-24: THIS IS A DEFECT, NOT INTENDED STRICTNESS.
# `if` and `case` are to be CONSISTENT and BOTH ACCEPTED. The inline case arm
# must take a statement exactly as the inline `if` body does.
#
# ⚠ LANE NOTE: the self-host accepts all eight cells of this family, so the
# self-host is the CORRECT lane here and Rust gg lags -- fix the Rust parser;
# never dumb the self-host down to match.
#
# ⚠ HOW IT WAS FOUND: an equivalence pair. Four spellings of one day-name
# lookup were run against each other; three worked and this one did not.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints Mon.
# Found 2026-08-24 by the robustness-map expansion; orchestrator-verified.

void main():
    String name = "?"
    int d = 1
    match d:
        case 1: name = "Mon"
        else: name = "other"
    print(name)
